<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">D'mt</h1><p class="st-meta">980 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.) · capital Yeha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The earliest known kingdom of the northern Ethiopian highlands, centred on the temple town of Yeha and closely tied to South Arabian Saba.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>D'mt emerged in the early first millennium BCE on the plateau of today's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tigray">Tigray</a> and highland <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a>. Its name appears in a handful of royal inscriptions, written in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> script, whose rulers took the title of mukarrib. Close contacts across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Sabaeans</a> shaped its writing, architecture and religion, though most scholars now stress local roots as well.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom is known mainly from monuments and inscriptions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-temple-of-yeha">Great Temple of Yeha</a>, built of finely dressed stone around the 7th century BCE, honoured the Sabaean moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaqah">Almaqah</a>. Farmers grew cereals, probably with ox-drawn ploughs, and used iron tools, while sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grat-beal-gebri">Grat Beal Gebri</a> show palace-like buildings. Its rulers and borders remain obscure.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>D'mt seems to have broken up into smaller polities around the 5th century BCE, and little is known of the centuries that followed. Its legacy of monumental building, writing and highland state-making passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a>, which rose in the same region from the 1st century CE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dmt.jpg" alt="D'mt" width="320" height="358"><figcaption>Image: El Bux · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%27mt_culture_at_its_height.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>980 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yeha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>South Arabian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sabaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Da'amat, Kingdom of D'mt</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-690&amp;focus=dmt" aria-label="D'mt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5748 1646 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where D'mt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6068 1902L6060 1908"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6060 1908h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6068 1902h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6068 1902h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yeha. <a href="/atlas?year=-690&amp;focus=dmt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
